\nLumos02111_XM_WZ_GR-15_NJP22033_CID-EThcD-MS2_3.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nReverse-phase liquid chromatography (RPLC) in Combination with Tip-Based Strong Cation Exchange (SCX-Tip) Fractionation, ReST, an Alternative Approach for Cross-linked Peptides analysis\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nChemical cross-linking in combination with mass spectrometry (XL-MS) has emerged as a useful method for structural elucidation of proteins and protein complexes. Efficient enrichment procedures are necessary to analyze cross-linked products due to their relatively low abundance. Currently, strong cation exchange chromatography (SCX), size exclusion chromatography (SEC), and affinity tag-based enrichment are among the widely used enrichment strategies. Herein, we present a two-dimensional enrichment strategy combining basic RPLC (bRPLC) fractionation and tip-based SCX (SCX-Tip) enrichment, termed ReST method, for the characterization of cross-linked peptides. We revealed the unbiased separation effects of the bRPLC in the cross-linked peptide fractionation. We optimized the enrichment conditions of SCX-Tip for cross-linked peptides using well-designed cross-linked peptides. Taking advantage of the high resolution of bRPLC fractionation and the high enrichment efficiency of SCX-Tip, we were able to identify 43.6% more cross-links than the conventional SCX approach. The presented ReST approach is an effective fractionation method for XL-MS analysis, and could be beneficial for protoeme-scale protein-protein interaction studies. Moreover, more stringent data filtering was proposed in order to achieve the high confidence of cross-linked peptide identification.\n\n[METHODS]\nHeLa cell culture, protein extraction, cross-linking and digestion HeLa (Human epithelial carcinoma) Cells (ATCC) were maintained in DMEM supplemented with 10% fetal bovine serum in humidified atmosphere with 5% CO2 at 37 °C and were grown as monolayer cultures in 10 cm tissue culture plate. HeLa cells were harvested by 2,000 g centrifugation at 4 °C for 10 min. The harvested cells were washed three times with PBS buffer and then resuspended in cross-linking buffer (20 mM HEPES, 150 mM NaCl, 1.5 mM MgCl2, 0.5 mM DTT, pH 7.8) containing 1× EDTA-free protease and phosphatase inhibitor cocktail (Thermo Fisher Scientific). The following preparation procedures of cross-linked peptides were the same as previously reported by Liu et al.18, 19 Briefly, the slurry solution was sonicated for 3 min (30 s sonication with 30 s intervals) in ice incubation, and the supernatant was collected by centrifugation at 13,800 g for 20 min at 4 °C. The protein concentration was determined using Bradford assay. Subsequently, the protein solution was diluted to 1 mg/mL with cross-linking buffer, incubated with 1 mM DSSO (dissolved in DMSO) for 1 h at room temperature, and quenched by adding 20 mM Tris-HCl, pH 8.0. Then the cross-linked samples were denatured with 8 M urea, reduced with 5 mM DTT at 37 °C for 30 min, alkylated with 15 mM IAM at 25 °C for 45 min in the dark. The excess DTT was removed by adding additional 5 mM DTT. Proteins were digestion by Lys-C (the ratio of enzyme to proteins as 1:50) for 4 h at 37 °C, after which the samples were diluted four times and followed by overnight digestion with trypsin (1:50) at 37 °C. The digestion was quenched with 1% FA. The digests were centrifuged at 20,000 g for 20 min at 4 °C, desalted using WondaSep C18 SPE columns (GL Sciences, Tokyo, Japan), and vacuum-dried for further application.  Strong cation exchange (SCX) fractionation Similarly, 1 mg of the desalted peptides were loaded onto the BioBasic SCX column (5 μm, 300 Å, 2.1 mm i.d. × 150 mm, Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA). SCX solvent A was consisted of 0.1% TFA in 30% ACN, solvent B was 0.1% TFA, 0.5 M NH4Cl in 30% ACN. The flow rate was set as 0.2 mL/min. The gradient was set as follows: 0-2% B in 0.01 min, 2-3% B in 8 min, 3-8% B in 6 min, 8-20% B in 13.99 min, 20-40% B in 10 min, 40-90% B in 10min, 90% B for 6 min, and 90-0% B for 6min. For each sample, a total of 34 fractions were collected and mixed into 10 fractions, desalted using home-made Poros R3 micro-columns, dried and stored at -20 °C for further use.  Reverse phase liquid chromatography (RPLC) fractionation RPLC fractionation was carried out on a Kinetex EVO C18 column (2.6 μm, 100 Å, 3 mm i.d. × 100 mm, Phenomenex, USA) using Dionex UltiMate-3000 HPLC system (Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA). The RPLC solvent A was 10 mM NH4HCO3, solvent B was 80% ACN containing 10 mM NH4HCO3. 1 mg of the generated peptides were loaded onto the column using mobile phase A, and the flow rate was set as 0.4 mL/min. The separation gradient was set as follows: 0% B for 5 min, 0-40% B in 25min, 40-100% B in 5 min, 100% B for 2 min, 100-0% B in 1 min, and 0% B for 7 min. Fractions were collected from the sixth minute with 1-min intervals in the next 30-min gradient, and finally the 30 fractions were mixed into 10 fractions for further use. Tip-based SCX fractionation Tip-based SCX fractionation of peptides with different charge states was performed according to Shema et al’s research28 with slight changes. A frit of C8 Empore Disc (3M) was added into a 200 μL pipet tip (Axygen). 10 mg of SCX particles (PolySULFOETHYL A, 5 μm particle size, 300 Å pore size, PolyLC, USA) were loaded onto the tip for each sample. The micro-column was conditioned with 100 μL of SCX buffer A (0.1% TFA, 30% ACN). The buffer was removed by centrifugation at 1,000 g for 8 min and discarded. Samples with 50 μg starting amount were re-solubilized in 100 μL of SCX buffer A, loaded onto the tip by centrifugation at 600 g for 15 min, and repeated once. For BSA peptides containing cross-linked peptides, SCX buffers with different salt concentrations were successively used to elute peptides with different charge states. Peptides were eluted by centrifugation at 1000 g for 8 min. Eluted fractions were collected individually for further MALDI-MS analysis. For HeLa cross-linked peptides, after loading, the microcolumn was washed once with 0.1% TFA, 100 mM NH4Cl in 30% ACN, and followed by elution with 0.5 M NH4HCO3 in 50% ACN. The elution was vaccum-dried to remove ACN, and then desalted using home-made Poros R3 microcolumns.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
