following preparation procedures of cross-linked peptides were the same as previously reported by Liu et al.18, 19 Briefly, the slurry solution was sonicated for 3 min (30 s sonication with 30 s intervals) in ice incubation, and the supernatant was collected by centrifugation at 13,800 g for 20 min at 4 °C. The protein concentration was determined using Bradford assay. Subsequently, the protein solution was diluted to 1 mg/mL with cross-linking buffer, incubated with 1 mM DSSO (dissolved in DMSO) for 1 h at room temperature, and quenched by adding 20 mM Tris-HCl, pH 8.0. Then the cross-linked samples were denatured with 8 M urea, reduced with 5 mM DTT at 37 °C for 30 min, alkylated with 15 mM IAM at 25 °C for 45 min in the dark. The excess DTT was removed by adding additional 5 mM DTT. Proteins were digestion by Lys-C (the ratio of enzyme to proteins as 1:50) for 4 h at 37 °C, after which the samples were diluted four times and followed by overnight digestion with trypsin (1:50) at 37 °C. The digestion was quenched with 1% FA. The digests were centrifuged at 20,000 g for 20 min at 4 °C, desalted using WondaSep C18 SPE columns (GL Sciences, Tokyo, Japan), and vacuum-dried for further application.  Strong cation exchange (SCX) fractionation Similarly, 1 mg of the desalted peptides were loaded onto the BioBasic SCX column (5 μm, 300 Å, 2.1 mm i.d. × 150 mm, Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA). SCX solvent A was consisted of 0.1% TFA in 30% ACN, solvent B was 0.1% TFA, 0.5 M NH4Cl in 30% ACN. The flow rate was set as 0.2 mL/min. The gradient was set as follows: 0-2% B in 0.01 min, 2-3% B in 8 min, 3-8% B in 6 min, 8-20% B in 13.99 min, 20-40% B in 10 min, 40-90% B in 10min, 90% B for 6 min, and 90-0% B for 6min. For each sample, a total of 34 fractions were collected and mixed into 10 fractions, desalted using home-made Poros R3 micro-columns, dried and stored at -20 °C for further use.  Reverse phase liquid chromatography (RPLC) fractionation RPLC fractionation was carried out on a Kinetex EVO C18 column (2.6 μm, 100 Å, 3 mm i.d. × 100 mm, Phenomenex, USA) using Dionex UltiMate-3000 HPLC system (Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA). The RPLC solvent A was 10 mM NH4HCO3, solvent B was 80% ACN containing 10 mM NH4HCO3. 1 mg of the generated peptides were loaded onto the column using mobile phase A, and the flow rate was set as 0.4 mL/min. The separation gradient was set as follows: 0% B for 5 min, 0-40% B in 25min, 40-100% B in 5 min, 100% B for 2 min, 100-0% B in 1 min, and 0% B for 7 min. Fractions were collected from the sixth minute with 1-min intervals in the next 30-min gradient, and finally the 30 fractions were mixed into 10 fractions for further use. Tip-based SCX fractionation Tip-based SCX fractionation of peptides with different charge states was performed according to Shema et al’s research28 with slight changes. A frit of C8 Empore Disc (3M) was added into a 200 μL pipet tip (Axygen). 10 mg of SCX particles (PolySULFOETHYL A, 5 μm particle size, 300 Å pore size, PolyLC, USA) were loaded onto the tip for each sample. The micro-column was conditioned with 100 μL of SCX buffer A (0.1% TFA, 30% ACN). The buffer was removed by centrifugation at 1,000 g for 8 min and discarded. Samples with 50 μg starting amount were re-solubilized in 100 μL of SCX buffer A, loaded onto the tip by centrifugation at 600 g for 15 min, and repeated once. For BSA peptides containing cross-linked peptides, SCX buffers with different salt concentrations were successively used to elute peptides with different charge states. Peptides were eluted by centrifugation at 1000 g for 8 min. Eluted fractions were collected individually for further MALDI-MS analysis. For HeLa cross-linked peptides, after loading, the microcolumn was washed once with 0.1% TFA, 100 mM NH4Cl in 30% ACN, and followed by elution with 0.5 M NH4HCO3 in 50% ACN. The elution was vaccum-dried to remove ACN, and then desalted using home-made Poros R3 microcolumns.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
